\section{Hockey-stick method (\code{method = "hockeystick"})}{

Fits the hockey-stick model (Danilenko et al., 2014) to the profile: the
profile is split into base, ascending and intermediate segments, the rise is
trimmed with the parallelogram rule, and the DLMO is the inflection point of
a linear base fit joined to a linear or parabolic rise fit, found by a
coarse and an optional fine grid search over the region of interest (ROI).

\strong{Arguments} (passed through \code{...}):
\itemize{
\item \code{threshold}: The numeric melatonin threshold for defining DLMO
(default: 2.3 pg/mL).
\item \code{interval_limit}: Numeric or lubridate duration. Indicates the time window
within which repeated melatonin threshold crossings are treated as part of
the same rise event. When multiple candidate rises occur within this
window, the later crossing is taken to represent the melatonin rise.
Single-point excursions above threshold that immediately return below
threshold are treated as blips and ignored automatically. Excursions with
more than one consecutive above-threshold point are treated as candidate
rises; if a later rise should be preferred, set \code{interval_limit} large
enough to include both candidate rise starts. The interval is measured
between the start times of candidate rises, not from when the earlier
excursion falls back below threshold. For example, if an early candidate
rise starts at 12:00 and a later sustained rise starts at 22:00, the
relevant interval is 10 hours. If provided as a numeric value, it
represents the window in hours (e.g., \code{interval_limit = 2} for 2 hours, or
\code{interval_limit = 0.5} for 30 minutes). Alternatively, users can specify a
\code{lubridate} duration object (e.g., \code{lubridate::hours(2)} for 2 hours,
\code{lubridate::minutes(30)} for 30 minutes). Default is 2 hours.
\item \code{fine_flag}: Logical. If \code{TRUE}, performs an additional fine-grid search
to refine the DLMO point after the initial coarse search (default: \code{TRUE}).
}

\strong{Value}: a list containing the following elements:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{prof}}: A tibble containing the segmented melatonin profile.
\itemize{
\item \code{datetime} (POSIXct): Timestamps of melatonin measurements.
\item \code{melatonin} (numeric): Melatonin concentration values.
\item \code{time} (hms): Time of day.
\item \code{slope} (numeric): Rate of change in melatonin concentration.
\item \code{base} (binary, 0/1): Indicates baseline segment.
\item \code{ascending} (binary, 0/1): Indicates ascending segment.
\item \code{intermediate} (binary, 0/1): if present, indicates intermediate segment.
}
\item \strong{\code{prl}}: List containing parallelogram rule fit paramters.
\itemize{
\item \code{pll_datetime_0} (POSIXct): Estimated lower bound of melatonin rise.
\item \code{pll_datetime_1} (POSIXct): Estimated upper bound of melatonin rise.
\item \code{pll_slope} (numeric): Slope of melatonin rise.
\item \code{corners} (list): Coordinates of parallelogram corners:
\itemize{
\item \code{ll}, \code{lr}, \code{ur}, \code{ul} (numeric vectors): (x, y) for each corner.
}
\item \code{flag} (logical): Whether parallelogram rule was violated or not.
}
\item \strong{\code{roi}}: List defining the Region of Interest (ROI).
\itemize{
\item \code{x_start} (POSIXct): Start time of the ROI window.
\item \code{x_end} (POSIXct): End time of the ROI window.
\item \code{y_min} (numeric): Minimum melatonin value in the ROI.
\item \code{y_max} (numeric): Maximum melatonin value in the ROI.
}
\item \strong{\code{threshold}}: Numeric. The melatonin threshold used for the DLMO calculation.
\item \strong{\code{interval_limit}}: lubridate duration. The interval-limit setting used
for repeated threshold-crossing selection.
\item \strong{\code{ip}}: List containing estimated DLMO inflection point.
\itemize{
\item \code{inflection_point} (tibble):
\itemize{
\item \code{x} (numeric): Estimated DLMO time index in units of decimal-hours.
\item \code{y} (numeric): Melatonin concentration at DLMO.
