\section{3k threshold method (\code{method = "3k"})}{

An individual threshold is set at the mean of the first \code{n_baseline} samples
plus \code{k_sd} standard deviations (Voultsios et al., 1997), and the DLMO is
the time at which melatonin rises above this threshold and stays above it
for at least \code{min_duration} hours, located by linear interpolation
(Molina & Burgess, 2011).

\strong{Arguments} (passed through \code{...}):
\itemize{
\item \code{n_baseline}: Integer (>= 2). Number of initial samples that form the
baseline (default 3).
\item \code{k_sd}: Numeric (>= 0). Number of baseline standard deviations added to
the baseline mean (default 2).
\item \code{min_duration}: Numeric (>= 0). Hours melatonin must stay above the
threshold for the crossing to count as the DLMO (default 2). Use 0 for the
first crossing.
\item \code{min_threshold}: Optional numeric. Lower bound for the threshold: if
mean + \code{k_sd} x SD is below \code{min_threshold}, \code{min_threshold} is used
instead (for example the assay limit of quantification). Useful when the
baseline sits at the assay floor and its SD is close to 0. Default \code{NULL}
(no lower bound, the original 3k method).
\item \code{lod}: Optional numeric. Assay limit of detection or quantification;
baseline values at or below it are reported as being at the assay floor.
Used for warnings only.
\item \code{max_gap}: Numeric (hours). A sampling gap longer than this within one
hour before to \code{min_duration} hours after the DLMO triggers a warning
(default 2).
\item \code{plot}: Logical. If \code{TRUE} (default), a diagnostic plot is added to the
result as \code{dlmoplot}.
}

The 3k method also accepts \code{data} with \code{time} (hms) instead of \code{datetime},
and CSV files that are comma or semicolon separated.

\strong{Value}: an object of class \code{dlmo_3k}, a list with
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{dlmo_time}} (hms) and \strong{\code{dlmo_datetime}} (POSIXct): the DLMO;
\item \strong{\code{status}}: \code{"ok"} (see the partial result attached to errors below);
\item \strong{\code{threshold}}: threshold used (pg/mL); \strong{\code{threshold_raw}}: mean + k_sd x SD
before \code{min_threshold};
\item \strong{\code{baseline}}: list with the baseline samples (\code{datetime}, \code{melatonin}),
their \code{mean}, \code{sd} and \code{n};
\item \strong{\code{intervals}}: tibble of all intervals above the threshold (\code{start}, \code{end},
\code{duration_h}, \code{open_end}, ...); \strong{\code{selected_interval}}: the row giving the DLMO;
\item \strong{\code{prof}}: the analysed profile with \code{hours_from_start}, \code{baseline} (0/1)
and \code{above_threshold} (0/1) columns;
\item \strong{\code{issues}}: data frame of the problems found (\code{code}, \code{severity},
\code{message}); \strong{\code{flags}}: named logical vector, one entry per issue code;
\item \strong{\code{settings}}: the arguments used;
\item \strong{\code{dlmoplot}}: \code{ggplot} object (if \code{plot = TRUE}).
}

\strong{Errors.} The function stops with an error when
\itemize{
\item the input is invalid: no input, invalid arguments, negative
concentrations, duplicated time stamps or fewer than \code{n_baseline + 2}
samples (see \code{\link[=check_3k_input]{check_3k_input()}});
\item no DLMO can be determined (see \code{\link[=select_dlmo_3k]{select_dlmo_3k()}}):
\itemize{
\item \code{no_crossing}: melatonin never rises above the threshold;
\item \code{not_sustained}: it rises above the threshold but never stays above it
for \code{min_duration} hours;
\item \code{truncated}: it rises above the threshold but the data end before
\code{min_duration} hours.
}
}

The "no DLMO" errors have class \code{dlmo_3k_error} and carry the partial result
as \code{result} (same elements as above, with \code{NA} DLMO, the failure \code{status}
and, if \code{plot = TRUE}, the plot), so the profile can still be inspected; see
the examples.

\strong{Warnings} (DLMO returned, but check it):
\code{baseline_sd_zero}, \code{baseline_at_floor} (see \code{\link[=check_3k_baseline]{check_3k_baseline()}}),
\code{gap_near_dlmo} (long sampling gap around the DLMO) and
\code{missing_values_removed}.

\strong{Notes} (messages): \code{unsorted_input}, \code{threshold_floored} (\code{min_threshold}
was used) and \code{transient_excursion} (shorter excursions above the threshold
before the DLMO were ignored).

A sample counts as above the threshold only when it is strictly greater than
the threshold, so a baseline with SD 0 (threshold equal to the baseline
value) is not mistaken for the rise; see \code{\link[=find_threshold_intervals]{find_threshold_intervals()}}.

Because the threshold comes from the first samples, the profile should start
in the daytime trough, a few hours before the expected onset. Profiles that
start during the morning decline or already on the rise give a biased
threshold; cut such profiles before calling this function.

See also \code{\link[=plot_profile_3k]{plot_profile_3k()}}, \code{\link[=compute_3k_threshold]{compute_3k_threshold()}},
\code{\link[=find_threshold_intervals]{find_threshold_intervals()}}, \code{\link[=select_dlmo_3k]{select_dlmo_3k()}} and \code{\link[=check_3k_baseline]{check_3k_baseline()}}.
}

\examples{
# ---- Hockey-stick method (default) ----
# Load the sample melatonin profile data included in the package
filename <- system.file("extdata/sample_melatonin_profile.csv", package = "dlmoR")

# Calculate the DLMO using the sample data and a threshold of 5
sample_dlmo <- calculate_dlmo(file_path = filename, threshold = 5)


# ---- 3k threshold method ----
filename <- system.file("extdata/sample_melatonin_profile.csv", package = "dlmoR")
sample_profile <- readr::read_delim(filename, delim = ";", show_col_types = FALSE)

# The sample profile starts at 07:30, during the morning decline. Start it in
# the afternoon trough so that the baseline is taken there:
afternoon <- sample_profile[sample_profile$datetime >=
                              as.POSIXct("2024-04-17 12:00:00", tz = "UTC"), ]
res <- calculate_dlmo(data = afternoon, method = "3k")
res
res$dlmoplot

# With the morning samples the threshold is inflated and no DLMO is found.
# The error carries the partial result, so the profile can still be plotted:
failed <- tryCatch(calculate_dlmo(data = sample_profile, method = "3k"),
                   dlmo_3k_error = function(e) e$result)
failed$status
failed$dlmoplot

}
